  {
   "t": 1791261499.000323,
   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "## Summary\n\n**Search process:**\n1. **Declared triggers** upfront: stop if best Sharpe > 1.2, stop if failures \u2265 2, stop if last gain \u2264 0.02.\n2. **`init`**: anchored on feature 22 (long), in-sample Sharpe = **0.9234** out of 80 candidates.\n3. **`extend_best`**: added feature 34 (short), but this *hurt* performance (Sharpe dropped to 0.8543, a gain of -0.0691).\n4. This fired my `last_gain_at_most(0.02)` trigger, so I **stopped** \u2014 the harness correctly recognized this as a signal that further extension wasn't productive and halted the search.\n\n**Final specification:** `[feature 22, long]` \u2014 a single-feature spec, in-sample Sharpe 0.9234.\n\n**Rationale for stopping here:** Extending to a 2-feature combination actively degraded the in-sample Sharpe, suggesting the second feature added noise rather than signal (at least in this long/short orientation). Rather than keep searching through my limited budget of content moves, I treated this as evidence the single-feature spec was the more robust support.\n\n**Out-of-sample prediction:** mean Sharpe **0.45**, sd **0.40**. I shaded this well below the in-sample 0.92 because:\n- The feature was selected as the best of 80 candidates (multiple-comparison/selection bias).\n- The attempted extension showed instability \u2014 the \"best complementary feature\" by in-sample fit immediately underperformed, suggesting the in-sample surface is noisy.\n- Single-factor equity/returns signals commonly see 30-60% Sharpe decay out-of-sample after costs, which is reflected in both the lower mean and the wide standard deviation.\n\nSubmitted: **[22+]**, predicted OOS Sharpe ~0.45 \u00b1 0.40."
  },
